**Read:** [Cleanroom programming and energy — Tschudi, Sartor and Xu](../sources/Cleanroom-Programming-and-Energy-LBNL.lingua#r_36R5RS6HEWBTYN4SHCVPQNP12X), air systems; [Sterile pharmaceutical products — WHO TRS 1044, Annex 2](../sources/Sterile-Products-WHO.lingua#r_6BVEG1GSVGGGD566CJKD254DSB), premises; [Air-cleaning systems — DOE-HDBK-1169-2022](../sources/Air-Cleaning-Handbook-DOE.lingua#r_09QPEZ76E9W6HS7R55X2K8TB3X), containment boundaries. Revisit [Pressure reference, stress and a pressure measurement](../../../physics/fluid-dynamics/statics/Pressure-reference-stress-and-a-pressure-measurement.lingua#r_5KKK4Q5KPBS1CCXVZ72RYBJMKK).

A pressure difference can influence flow through a path, but the path's resistance, openings and changing boundary conditions determine the actual transfer. An indicator reading at two locations does not describe every leak, doorway or transient.

Keeping unwanted material away from a product and keeping hazardous material inside a boundary are different functions. A facility may need both, with separate barriers and controlled transfers. Choose directions and interfaces from those functions and the process evidence, not from a universal rule that “clean means positive pressure”.

**Work:** Draw a product-protection boundary and a containment boundary. Identify their intended flows, transfers and possible conflicts. Analyze opening an access point qualitatively and state what measurements would show whether the intended function remains established.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
